.TH JPEGENC 2
.SH NAME
jpegenc, jpegplanes \- I420 video frames to JPEG and back
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "jpeg.m";
enc := load Jpegenc Jpegenc->PATH;
jp := load Jpegplanes Jpegplanes->PATH;

encode:	fn(w, h: int, y, u, v: array of byte, quality: int):
		(array of byte, string);
planes:	fn(d: array of byte):
		(int, int, array of byte, array of byte, array of byte, string);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
These convert between video frames in planar YCbCr 4:2:0 (I420), as
vid9p and
.IR camsrv (4)
serve them, and JPEG, without passing through RGB either way.
.PP
.B Encode
makes a baseline JPEG (JFIF, three components, Y sampled 2x2 and Cb and
Cr 1x1) of a
.IR w x h
frame:
.I y
holds
.IR w * h
samples, and
.I u
and
.I v
each
.RI ( w +1)/2
by
.RI ( h +1)/2.
.I Quality
from 1 to 100 scales the quantisation tables of ITU T.81 Annex K as
libjpeg scales them, so a quality means what it does there; the Huffman
tables are Annex K's.  A width or height that is not a multiple of 16 is
padded by repeating the last column or row.  The same frame gives the
same bytes.  It returns the JPEG, or nil and the reason if the planes are
too short for the size, or the size or quality is out of range.
.PP
.B Planes
decodes a JPEG of that form (three components, the first sampled 2x2 and
the others 1x1) and returns its width, height and Y, Cb and Cr planes as
decoded, before the upsampling and colour conversion an RGB image needs,
or the reason it cannot.
.B Jpegplanes
is implemented by
.B readjpg
.RI ( imagefile (2)),
so it decodes as that does: baseline, extended and progressive JPEG, and
libjpeg's integer inverse DCT.
.PP
A 480x270 frame encodes in about 25 ms on a Raspberry Pi 5 under the JIT
compiler, and decodes in about 3 ms on an Apple M-series machine.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/jpegenc.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/readjpg.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR videnc (1),
.IR i420 (2),
.IR imagefile (2)
